From 3530b04ffa7c292bad9711995c397408193de950 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Brue <ryanbrue.dev@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 01:23:50 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 30/35] PROTOTYPE pco: fold a constant LOD bias into the
 sampler state

A sample with an LOD bias passes the bias per pixel (lod_mode bias,
pplod), and the texture unit then takes about 2.5x as long per sample
as with the hardware's own per-quad LOD. Explicit gradients cost the
same. Skia (Chromium's compositor) samples with a constant -0.475 bias,
so on a GX6250 Angelfish's compositor pass for a 720p video took 27 ms
instead of 6.

For a constant bias, add it to the sampler's own LOD bias instead
(dadjust: 5.8 fixed point, bits 0..12 of the first sampler word; its
+-16 range is the clamp Vulkan applies to the sum of both biases), and
sample with normal LOD. Sample instructions read texture and sampler
state from shared registers, so the modified words are computed by the
preamble:

- nir_opt_preamble may move load_{tex,smp}_state_pco (they are uniform
  for a uniform index); pco gives them no cost of their own, so only
  values computed from them are hoisted.
- pco keeps a preamble value used only as texture/sampler state a
  vector (4-aligned), and lower_smp takes each state from either its
  descriptor or the preamble.
- pco_opt_prop_hw_comps turns a comp from a hw vector into a hw
  register (the preamble storing a state word) into a mov.

Only samples in the function's top-level control flow with constant
texture and sampler indices are folded, so the preamble can compute
the state. If it still did not (out of preamble registers), lower_smp
uses the descriptor's state and warns once: the bias is lost, not the
draw.

Images match the per-pixel bias exactly except where the bias is not a
multiple of 1/256 and a trilinear filter blends two levels: at most 1
in 255 in a channel.

